{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let Reuben live.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): let him pray for him that he lengthen his days and not die before the appointed years, for it is impossible that he live forever." "<b>And let his men be.</b> That is, ואל יהי מתיו — 'and let not his men die'; and similar is (Proverbs 30:3) ״ולא למדתי חכמה ודעת קדושים אדע״, and likewise (Psalms 9:19) ״כי לא לנצח ישכח אביון תקות עניים תאבד לעד״, and likewise (Psalms 38) ״ובחמתך תיסרני״, and likewise (Isaiah 42) ״ותהלתי לפסילים״." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses our master, peace be upon him, mentioned them in order according to the arrangement of their inheritance. For Reuben took his inheritance first, and because he was the firstborn, therefore he gave him in his blessing the right of the firstborn. And he set Judah after him, for he is the first to inherit in the land, and he will go up first to the battle in which Moses blesses them. And after Judah he blessed the sons of Levi, for they encamp in Jerusalem together with the sons of Judah. And after the sons of Levi he blessed Benjamin, whose inheritance was together with the sons of Judah, and the city of Jerusalem and the Temple were between Judah and Benjamin, and the Levites encamped with both of them. And after Benjamin, Joseph — lest he delay him, for he had already set Benjamin before him though he is younger than he. And after Joseph, Zebulun, for he wages war and brings provision to Issachar; and because Zebulun is the cause of Issachar's Torah, therefore he set Zebulun first and after him Issachar. And after the sons of the matriarchs he mentioned Gad, and began with him because he belongs to the standard of the sons of the matriarchs. And after Gad he mentioned Dan, for he is master of a standard. And afterwards he mentioned Naphtali, who is older than Asher, and therefore he needed to set him later." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret יחי ראובן ואל ימות — 'Let Reuben live and not die' — as: may he succeed and live in his wars, and may not one of them die in battle. ויהי מתיו מספר — 'and let his men be a number' — but let them be included among the ten slain, namely the ten martyrs of the kingdom; for we find that ten are called מספר, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 10:19) ״מספר יהיו ונער יכתבם״ — and how many is 'a number' in the writing of a child, which is a single point. And according to this it appears that the matter of Bilhah was atoned for him, in that he would not be cut off and die forever on its account; but it was not fully atoned, for he is destined to be punished for it among the ten martyrs of the kingdom who sinned in the sale of Joseph." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> יחי ראובן ואל ימות — 'Let Reuben live and not die' — he mentioned יחי for the life of the world to come, and אל ימות, that he not return from there again in a body to die a second death. And this is what Onkelos, peace be upon him, translated: ומותא תנינא לא ימות — 'and the second death he shall not die.' And he revealed to us thereby that the souls transmigrate to return in a body after they have received their reward and punishment in the Garden of Eden or in Gehenna. And this is known and received: just as the sabbatical cycles of the world revolve and cycle, so the soul, after receiving its reward in the Garden of Eden or its punishment in Gehenna, returns after a time in a body to receive what is fitting for it, measure for measure. And this transmigration is called by our Rabbis of blessed memory תחיית המתים — the revival of the dead. That is what they said at the beginning of tractate Ta'anit: 'Granted that the revival of the dead has its time every single day, but rain — does it have its time every single day?' And you will find in the Yerushalmi (Isaiah 22): ״אם יכופר העון הזה לכם עד תמותון״ — this is the second death; and likewise Jonathan translated: 'If this sin is forgiven you until you die the second death.' And to this Onkelos directed his intention and expanded the explanation in the language he used: לחיי עלמא ומותא תניינא לא ימות — 'to the life of eternity, and the second death he shall not die.' He intended to say that Moses prayed for the tribe that it merit to live eternal life and not return from there again in a body to die a second death. And likewise you will find in David explicitly that he mentioned, regarding the soul, the matters that come to it after death: forgiveness of sins, healing of illnesses in its transmigration, redemption from the pit; and this teaches that it passes through the pit, but He will transmigrate it after its adornment with the attributes of satiety of joys in the radiance of the Shekhinah. That is what he said (Psalms 103:3–4): ״הסולח לכל עונכי הרופא לכל תחלואיכי״, ״הגואל משחת חייכי המעטרכי חסד ורחמים״, ״המשביע בטוב עדיך תתחדש כנשר נעוריכי״. And there are five benefits that he mentioned with the addition of the five yods, and they are five corresponding to five things with which it is compared, and corresponding to five names that it has. And this is explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "He reads the vav of ויהי conversively ('and let not'), citing parallel cases where ולא/לא governs clauses where a vav-consecutive appears; verse references normalized from his abbreviated citations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:08.336756196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}